---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navigation from '../../../components/Navigation.astro';
import PageHero from '../../../components/sections/PageHero.astro';
import GuideDownload from '../../../components/sections/GuideDownload.astro';
import FAQSection from '../../../components/sections/FAQSection.astro';
import RelatedReading from '../../../components/sections/RelatedReading.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import GuideByline from '../../../components/sections/GuideByline.astro';
import { GUIDES } from '../../../data/guides';
import { PERSON_ALESIA } from '../../../data/people';

const guide = GUIDES.po;

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Resources', href: '/resources/' },
  { label: 'Guides', href: '/resources/guides/' },
  { label: 'AI for Product Owners', href: '/resources/guides/ai-for-product-owners/' },
];

// Every figure here repeats one already sourced inside the guide.
const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'What should a product owner never paste into Copilot?',
    answer: 'Three questions settle most cases in a second. Which account am I signed into, work or personal? A personal account is a different product with different terms. Would I put this in an email to a supplier? Is it someone else\'s personal data, such as user research recordings or support tickets with names in them? Your company policy overrides all three.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What does AI actually do in backlog refinement?',
    answer: 'It does the part you should have settled before the session. It will list the questions a developer would ask before picking an item up, propose splits, and draft acceptance criteria that are testable rather than a restatement of the title. It moves time rather than removing it: refinement reaches the questions worth having sooner.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can AI prioritize or order a product backlog?',
    answer: 'No, and it should not be asked to. The Scrum Guide places ordering with the product owner, who stays accountable for it. A model that has never met your users is guessing, however fluently it writes. Use AI to argue against an order you already hold, never to produce one.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does Copilot give a different answer to the same prompt?',
    answer: 'Microsoft states in its own guidance that the same prompt used several times can return different responses. One good answer therefore does not prove the prompt was good. Keep a prompt only once it has produced a usable answer more than once, on different inputs.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is the guide free, and do you email the file?',
    answer: 'It is free and the download opens straight away. We do not email the file to you, and downloading starts no sales sequence. Besides your email and role, the form asks which kind of help would be useful, and your answer decides nothing about the download.',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="AI for Product Owners: Backlog Refinement Guide | Ironum"
  description={`Copilot probably can't read your Jira. What AI can still do for refinement and acceptance criteria, and what stays yours. Free ${guide.pages.replace(' pages', '-page')} guide.`}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  ogImage="/images/og/og-ai-product-owners.jpg"
  structuredData={[{
    "@type": "DigitalDocument",
    "name": guide.title,
    "numberOfPages": parseInt(guide.pages),
    "author": { "@id": "https://ironum.com/#person-alesia" },
    "publisher": { "@id": "https://ironum.com/#organization" },
    "encodingFormat": "application/pdf",
    "isAccessibleForFree": true,
    "inLanguage": "en",
  }, PERSON_ALESIA]}
>
  <Navigation cta={{ text: 'Get the free guide', href: '#get-the-guide' }} />
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="FREE GUIDE"
    title="AI for Product Owners"
    subtitle="Your Copilot probably can't read your backlog. Here is what AI can still do for refinement, and where it stops."
    ctaText="Get the free guide"
    ctaLink="#get-the-guide"
    showAvatar={false}
  />

  <section class="px-4 md:px-20 py-20 md:py-32">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-8 text-gray-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-black leading-snug">If your backlog lives in Jira or Azure DevOps, no version of Microsoft Copilot reads it on its own. Most product owners find that out the hard way, after deciding the tool is overrated.</p>
      <p>Nobody taught them otherwise. In Germany, where it has been measured, only 27% of workplaces already using generative AI train anyone in it, and only 21% have written down any rules for using it. This guide follows the product owner's work as the Scrum Guide describes it, names what AI does well in each part, and says plainly where it stops.</p>
      <GuideByline sources="Germany's federal employment research institute (IAB), Eurostat, the OECD, the Scrum Guide and Microsoft's own documentation" />
      <a href="#get-the-guide" class="inline-block bg-blue-600 text-white rounded-full px-6 py-4 font-medium text-lg hover:bg-blue-700 transition-all duration-200 min-h-[48px]">Get the free guide</a>

      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-black pt-8">What AI backlog refinement can and cannot do</h2>
      <p>AI backlog refinement works on the parts you should have settled before the session. It will list the questions a developer would ask before picking an item up, and draft acceptance criteria that are testable rather than a restatement of the title. It cannot order your backlog and it cannot decide what is valuable. That judgment is the job, and writing a good user story stays teamwork.</p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-black pt-8">What the guide says about Copilot and Jira</h2>
      <p>Which Copilot tier can see your work decides everything else, and the Jira connector often returns nothing even once it is set up. The guide gives you the tier table and the workaround. For the short version, read <a href="/resources/blog/ai-backlog-refinement-product-owners/" class="text-blue-600 underline">AI backlog refinement when Copilot can't read your Jira</a>.</p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-black pt-8">What is inside</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 list-none p-0">
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-orange-500 font-bold">01</span> Six things nobody told you, from the license nobody checks to where your prompts actually go</li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-orange-500 font-bold">02</span> Four habits that separate method from luck</li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-orange-500 font-bold">03</span> The five things that are actually yours: writing items, refinement, ordering, sprint planning and review</li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-orange-500 font-bold">04</span> A prompt pack in Microsoft's four-part structure: goal, context, expectations, source</li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-orange-500 font-bold">05</span> A thirty minute weekly routine that needs no budget and nobody's approval</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-black pt-8">When the limit is the tool, not you</h2>
      <p>Most of the guide is method, and method is yours to fix. Some limits are not. When a model can read your backlog directly, it can check every item against the Product Goal, find duplicates across the whole backlog rather than the twenty items you pasted, and refine a story against its real history, while respecting who may see which project. That is called grounding, and it does not have to mean sending your data abroad.</p>
      <p>This is the work Ironum does: <a href="/services/enterprise-rag/" class="text-blue-600 underline">private RAG</a> and <a href="/services/custom-ai-development/" class="text-blue-600 underline">custom AI</a> connected to the systems a product team already uses, Jira and Confluence included, hosted in the EU or on your own infrastructure.</p>
      <p>If you have hit one of these limits, write to Alesia with the limit and the system your backlog lives in. She replies herself, within two working days, with whether it is solvable and roughly what it would take. No sales sequence follows.</p>
      <a href="/contact/?source=guide-po" class="inline-block border-2 border-blue-600 text-blue-600 rounded-full px-6 py-4 font-medium text-lg hover:bg-blue-50 transition-all duration-200 min-h-[48px]">Ask whether your limit is solvable</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <GuideDownload guideId="po" />
  <FAQSection items={faqItems} title="AI for product owners: common questions" />
  <RelatedReading
    eyebrow="KEEP READING"
    heading="More on AI and your backlog"
    cta="Read more"
    links={[
      {
        href: '/resources/blog/ai-backlog-refinement-product-owners/',
        title: 'AI Backlog Refinement When Copilot Can\'t Read Jira',
        description: 'What to paste, what must never go in, and the refinement work AI genuinely does.',
      },
      {
        href: '/resources/guides/ai-for-product-managers/',
        title: 'AI for Product Managers',
        description: 'The companion guide: where AI changes the product, not just the pace.',
      },
      {
        href: '/services/enterprise-rag/',
        title: 'Enterprise RAG',
        description: 'AI that answers from your own Jira, Confluence and documents, deployed on infrastructure you control.',
      },
    ]}
  />
  <Footer />
</Layout>
